---
/**
 * The homepage hero AS SHIPPED (#27): the film full bleed, and every word the
 * visitor has to read on one navy panel offset into its bottom-right, hanging
 * past the film onto the amber rule.
 *
 * It is a component rather than markup inside `index.astro` for one reason: the
 * client asked to see this and the PREVIOUS hero side by side in two tabs
 * (2026-08-27), so the two are now `HeroBleed` and `HeroFramed`, the page body
 * they share is `HomeSections`, and `/` and `/hero-original/` are two thin pages
 * that pick a hero. Nothing about this hero changed in that move.
 *
 * The reasoning, the measured ratios and the arithmetic that ties the overhang to
 * the trust strip's padding are on the blocks below, next to the classes they
 * explain.
 */
import BookButton from '../venue/BookButton.astro';
import HeroFilm from '../venue/HeroFilm.astro';
import { buttonClasses } from '../../lib/buttons';
import lakevilleNightPoster from '../../assets/images/lakeville-night-poster.jpg';
---

{
  /* 1. HERO — the room at night, FULL BLEED, with everything the visitor has to
      read on one panel offset into its bottom-right. Promise + what it is + where,
      one primary CTA. Venue-neutral by design: the venue question is asked at the
      Book click, not the front door. The subhead carries an 8px amber slab rather
      than an opacity — the block this replaced set its eyebrow at opacity-70 and
      its subhead at opacity-80, both of which re-derive their own contrast against
      whatever ground they land on.

      **The film is the hero** (FW-4010), which FW-4003 declined — and the way it
      is declined matters, because the refusal was measured and the measurement was
      right. What it measured was ONE structure: display type composited over
      moving footage, which needs a 71% scrim on the shipping clip and leaves about
      11% of its luminance range. Nothing is composited here: every word sits on an
      OPAQUE navy panel, so contrast is a token pairing again (paper 12.24:1, amber
      7.21:1, both computed) and the film runs unscrimmed. The re-measurement on
      the file that ships is in `HeroFilm`.

      **Full bleed, and the panel now carries the whole hero** (2026-08-26, the
      human's call). This takes the hero treatment — and only the hero treatment —
      from the `home-fhm-full` divergence candidate. Nothing else came with it: the
      page keeps its own palette, its sentence case, its square panels everywhere
      else and its 3px controls, and `radius.media` is the one new token, scoped to
      this hero in its own description.

      What full bleed costs is in `HeroFilm`: the film stops being a framed screen
      and becomes a ground, it loses the 2px rule that made it read as a screen
      rather than a hole, and `object-fit: cover` crops it vertically. The panel
      then occludes part of what is left. Both are real losses on footage the
      client paid for, and both were chosen over the alternative — a scrim — which
      costs an order of magnitude more of the same thing.

      DOM order is eyebrow → h1 → words → buttons → film, on every viewport. The
      overlap is a desktop-only layer over that order, so reading order and tab
      order never diverge, and on mobile the panel stops being a panel at all: no
      fill, no rule, no radius, the film below the CTAs. That last part is
      load-bearing rather than tidiness — the mobile page-weight budget for `/` is
      350 KB and it holds only because the film is BELOW THE FOLD there and the
      IntersectionObserver never fetches it. A background video in the mobile hero
      fetches ~950 KB on arrival and fails `audit:visual` on the visitors least
      able to afford it. */
}
<section class="on-dark bg-midnight pt-s8 pb-s7 lg:pt-0 lg:pb-0">
  {
    /* Panel and film share ONE grid cell at lg. Three things about this shape
        are deliberate:

        1. **The panel sizes the row; the film fills it.** The panel is
           `self-start`, so it keeps its natural height and the row takes that
           height; the film is a stretched grid item at `h-full` and covers into
           it. That inversion is what makes full bleed safe at every width — sized
           by aspect ratio instead, a 1920-wide viewport gives a 1080px-tall hero,
           and at 1024 the panel's own content is taller than 16:9 leaves room for,
           so the panel would overflow upward out of the band. Letting the words
           set the height removes both failures at once. `min-h` is the floor for
           the case where the copy is ever short.
        2. **It hangs below the film.** `translate-y` moves the panel down out of
           the row without changing the row's height, so the film ends where the
           row ends and the panel carries on past it onto the midnight band. The
           section's `pb-s10` (7rem) is what that 3.5rem of overhang is falling
           into — they are a pair, and shrinking the padding clips the panel.
        3. **The edge survives the whole clip, in both directions.** `navy` on
           `midnight` is 1.31:1, so the fill alone has no edge on the band below,
           and a paper rule alone is 1.43:1 against the film's brightest block. The
           two are complementary and the panel carries both: the fill holds the
           edge where the footage is bright (8.58:1 against the brightest block)
           and the 2px paper rule holds it where the footage is near-black
           (19.31:1) and against the midnight band itself (16.09:1). Computed
           against the block luminances measured on the shipping file.

        `z-raised` rather than DOM order: the panel comes FIRST in the markup so
        mobile reads correctly, which would otherwise let the film paint over it. */
  }
  <div class="lg:grid lg:grid-cols-1">
    <div
      class="mx-auto max-w-content px-gutter lg:z-raised lg:col-start-1 lg:row-start-1 lg:mx-0 lg:flex lg:max-w-none lg:translate-y-s10 lg:justify-end lg:self-end lg:px-gutter-lg"
    >
      <div
        class="lg:w-full lg:max-w-[44rem] lg:rounded-media lg:border-rule lg:border-paper lg:bg-navy lg:p-s7 xl:max-w-[52rem]"
      >
        <p class="caps-label text-amber">Lakeville &amp; Stillwater, Minnesota</p>
        {
          /* THE CLIENT'S OWN HEADLINE, restored verbatim from their live homepage
              (`_ingest/raw/home.html`) and approved for reuse 2026-08-04 (FW-4000).
              Ours was "Indoor golf that's actually a night out." — which is fine,
              and theirs is better, because it is the sentence they already say
              about themselves. The subhead keeps the night-out job, so nothing is
              lost.

              Inside the panel it is `text-hero` still, so the page's largest
              gesture stays the page's largest gesture — the panel is 8 of 12
              columns precisely so the h1 has the measure to survive at that step
              rather than shrinking to fit a narrower box. */
        }
        <h1 class="mt-s4 text-hero-compact leading-display font-extrabold tracking-display-tight text-white">
          The most realistic indoor golf you can play, period.
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-s5 max-w-prose-page border-l-slab border-amber pl-s4 text-body leading-body text-paper">
          Ten GolfZon bays across two Twin Cities venues. Book by the hour, bring up to six people for the same price,
          and let the simulator keep score while you order another round.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-s6 flex flex-wrap gap-s4">
          <BookButton size="lg" variant="onDark" />
          <a href="/rates/" class={buttonClasses('lg', 'ghostDark')}>See rates &amp; hours</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-s6 lg:relative lg:col-start-1 lg:row-start-1 lg:mt-0 lg:min-h-[34rem]">
      <HeroFilm
        bleed
        name="lakeville-night"
        poster={lakevilleNightPoster}
        alt="Silent video: a group of friends around a table with drinks and food in the Lakeville room, then a player stepping up and hitting a shot into a lit simulator screen."
      />
    </div>
  </div>

  {
    /* The band the panel hangs past, as a 1rem AMBER stripe rather than 7rem of
        midnight (2026-08-26). Three notes on it:

        - **Amber, not ember.** Ember is the obvious "third colour" and it is the
          wrong one: the page spends its ember band ONCE, on the featured offer, and
          `blue-navy`'s whole argument is that a second one spends the only heat on
          the page. Amber is already the hero's accent — the eyebrow and the
          subhead's slab — so the stripe closes that gesture instead of opening a
          new one. It carries no text, so `rules.noAmberOnLight` (which governs
          amber as INK on a light ground) does not reach it.
        - **1rem, and the height is the whole point.** At 7rem it was a section; at
          1rem it is a rule under the film. `space.s4` is the one value to change.
        - **The panel is meant to cross it, and the section below moves down to
          keep its distance.** It hangs `s10` (7rem) past the film — 112px, which is
          80px more than it used to and about 15% of the 544px film box (requested
          2026-08-27, 'another 15%'). What it has to clear is the locations
          section's 4px ember score rule, and THAT is why three values move together
          rather than one: the rule sits at its own section's top padding, so the
          clearance is `stripePadding + trustStripPaddingTop - translate`. Hold that
          expression at 24px and the ramp does the rest — `s5` + `s10` - `s10` = 24.
          The stripe going 1rem -> 1.5rem is what keeps all three on the space ramp
          instead of one of them landing on an off-scale 5.5rem.

          The gap is 24px at 1024, 1440 and 1920, and it is the thing the next nudge
          downward spends first; the hours are what it spends second, and they are
          the highest-leverage block on the page. The panel's edge survives the
          crossing without help: navy on white is 13.31:1, so the fill does the work
          the paper rule cannot on that side. */
  }
  <div class="hidden lg:block lg:h-s5 lg:bg-amber"></div>
</section>
